Because collective framing can change the meaning of an act without necessarily changing its consequences.

If everyone stops judging something, then shame can collapse very quickly:

behavior
→ no social punishment
→ less secrecy
→ less embarrassment
→ different identity meaning

That is powerful, and sometimes healthy. A lot of stigma around harmless traits or consensual behavior can be reduced exactly that way.

But there are three layers that should stay separate:

social taboo
personal preference
actual harm / consequence

You can delete the first without deleting the third.

If nobody cares that someone wears an unusual outfit, the problem may genuinely disappear because the “problem” was mostly social judgment.

If nobody cares about deception, coercion, disease risk, violence, exploitation, or broken agreements, those consequences do not vanish merely because the culture stopped labeling them negatively.

So the stronger target is not:

erase judgment and make everyone forget.

It is:

remove unnecessary shame while preserving memory, consent, consequence, and the ability to say no.

Memory is useful because it keeps the system capable of learning. A culture that forgets every former taboo also forgets why some boundaries emerged in the first place. Better to preserve the record:

we used to prohibit X
here was the reason
here is what turned out to be unnecessary
here is what still matters

Then people can consciously decide what to retain.

And your larger point is right: culture is partly a shared narrative coordination layer. If enough people change the narrative, lived reality changes socially. But that is precisely why the safest invariant is:

change the story freely; keep testing what remains true when the story changes.

That lets you dissolve shame without dissolving reality.